1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,600
So imagine spending, uh, five years and $50 million building this massive digital public service in your ministry.

2
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,660
Right. A huge investment.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:15,180
Exactly. Only for a newly elected cabinet to just, you know, scrap the entire thing because they couldn't see any immediate results.

4
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,799
Oh, well, it happens constantly in government.

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:17,919
It really does.

6
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:28,100
So today we are doing a deep dive into a policy briefing on Progressive Phase Four: plan to, well, figure out how to prevent that exact

7
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:28,740
disaster.

8
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,540
Yeah. It's arguably one of the most complex tightrope walks in government tech.

9
00:00:32,629 --> 00:00:40,060
Yeah. I mean, you have to balance this incredibly dense technical execution with, uh, very urgent political promises.

10
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:45,570
Because when ministries fail at this, they end up with systems that are just completely fragmented- So-... duplicated or siloed, right?

11
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:52,340
Exactly. And then data is constantly being rekeyed by hand, and the whole infrastructure just becomes incredibly costly to change.

12
00:00:52,540 --> 00:01:01,660
So our mission today is to break down how you actually move from vague decisions to a sequenced, honestly costed roadmap, and doing all of that within just, like,

13
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:03,750
six to eight weeks of planning.

14
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:11,300
Right. And the briefing anchors this entire process around the PAERA framework or PR.

15
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:18,880
Okay, before we get too deep into the weeds here, what exactly is a Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture?

16
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:21,380
Think of a reference architecture as,

17
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:25,800
um, the master digital city grid for government.

18
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:31,580
It's this standardized blueprint that dictates exactly how different IT systems should communicate.

19
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:41,600
Okay. And what makes the PAERA framework so effective is how it actually sequences this massive roadmap into four distinct waves rather than just doing one

20
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:42,500
giant rollout.

21
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:49,540
Well, I have to push back on that a little bit because I'd imagine building a national system in disjointed pieces is, you know, pretty risky.

22
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:55,920
How so? Well, if you're building a skyscraper, you don't just build the lobby, use it for a month, and then start pouring concrete for the rest of the foundation later, right?

23
00:01:55,980 --> 00:02:00,660
Right. Why not just build the whole digital infrastructure at once to ensure everything is perfectly in step?

24
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,640
Because software isn't physical, right, and government funding definitely isn't guaranteed.

25
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:11,520
That's fair. If you spend five years just building invisible groundwork, you know, laying the digital concrete,

26
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:14,720
you are gonna lose your budget and your political support.

27
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:20,980
Taxpayers and ministers need to see their money actually doing something.

28
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:21,800
Ah, I see.

29
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:30,760
So Wave One, which they call Inception, focuses on standing up the repository in the EA board and making the learner register authoritative.

30
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:35,300
Okay. Let me just translate that into plain English to make sure I'm following the mechanics here.

31
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:40,180
The repository is basically the central catalog of digital building blocks, right?

32
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,100
That's exactly. And the board governs the rules.

33
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:50,640
But making a register authoritative, that means establishing a single source of truth so other departments stop keeping their own conflicting spreadsheets.

34
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:56,359
Precisely. You establish the rules, and that single source of truth in Wave One, that's the invisible plumbing.

35
00:02:56,520 --> 00:03:01,480
Right. But then Wave Two immediately delivers a high priority use case, so the public actually sees a win.

36
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:03,340
What does that look like in practice?

37
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:08,240
Well, in this brief, that means delivering a single learner record inside the first year.

38
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:13,180
So the examination authority and the grant program, they stop keeping their own separate lists.

39
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:22,040
Mechanically, that is huge. Instead of three departments digging their own separate wells, they are all forced to draw water from that one authoritative learner register.

40
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:30,140
Yes. And that visible win guarantees the momentum for Wave Three, which the brief calls the shared data exchange backbone.

41
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:35,420
And that backbone is what? The standardized digital piping that securely connects all these different databases?

42
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:41,560
Exactly. And once you have that shared backbone running, you finally reach Wave Four, which is mass scale.

43
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:46,320
Every other education service can now just plug right into the existing infrastructure.

44
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:56,620
I see the logic now. So the minister gets to proudly announce this single learner record in year one while the IT architects secure the time and infrastructure they actually need for year

45
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:57,140
five.

46
00:03:57,150 --> 00:03:58,320
That's the balance, yeah.

47
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:02,670
But executing this brings us to, um, arguably the hardest part, the money.

48
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,440
Costing this must be just so difficult. Oh, absolutely.

49
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,040
Because if you ask for five years of funding up front, the cabinet might just laugh you out of the room.

50
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:13,630
But if you try to fund it annually,

51
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:18,560
you risk losing your vendor or getting locked into one supplier.

52
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:21,500
How does this plan actually solve that funding trap?

53
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:29,400
By using directional cost estimates to secure a multi-year envelope from the cabinet rather than fighting for exact line items every twelve months.

54
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:37,620
Oh, interesting. Yeah. So Wave One mostly funds human capital, so the initial engagement in setting up a permanent National Enterprise Architecture

55
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:43,810
or EA team. Then Waves Two and Three fund the actual integration work and backbone investment.

56
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:51,040
Okay. Securing the multi-year budget is great, but I keep thinking about that classic communication breakdown between the business side and the IT side.

57
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:52,160
Right. It's a huge issue.

58
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:58,080
You have a minister who speaks in policy promises and an IT architect who speaks in data sequences.

59
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:03,350
If they are looking at the exact same roadmap, how do they not just talk past each other?

60
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:08,540
Because the roadmap is quite literally designed as a single page that you can read two different ways.

61
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:11,180
The minister reads the outcomes and the dates.

62
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:15,020
So they see, like, single learner record delivered by next year.

63
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:16,220
Exactly. Mm.

64
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:21,300
But the architect looks at that exact same page and reads the technical dependencies.

65
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,599
They see Wave Two cannot start until the learner register is made authoritative in Wave One.

66
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:34,760
That is brilliant actually. The political promise and the technical reality are physically locked together on one sheet of paper.

67
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:41,520
No translation required. Yep. Which brings us to the final gate of this entire planning phase, Sign-off Four.

68
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:46,860
Right. So roughly six months from the first day of discovery, you hit this most consequential step.

69
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:53,340
The decision maker and the EA board formally approve that dual read roadmap and commit the multi-year budget.

70
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:57,480
And that document is the exact deliverable your minister takes to the cabinet.

71
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:03,020
That's it. The blueprint is locked, the money is secured, and the project shifts entirely from planning to building.

